WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.105
"Green Tea as a Mouthwash
for Halitosis (Bad Breath)"

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.840
Tea is the second most widely
consumed beverage in the world,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.000
after water, to the tune of
billions of pounds every year.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.560
In my last video I showed
how drinking green tea

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.080
after eating garlic
helps deodorize your breath.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.360
But what about drinking
green tea

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.600
to deodorize regular bad breath
without the garlic?

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.680
There's been a lot of studies
on the effect of green tea

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.200
on other aspects
of oral health.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.400
For example, green tea
appears to work as well

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.800
as chlorhexidine
for reducing plaque,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.400
and chlorhexidine
is like the gold standard.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.400
Green tea is safer too.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.080
Chlorhexidine has
so many side effects

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:42.840
you're not supposed to use

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.000
if for more than
a short time period;

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.160
side effects like discoloration
of the teeth,

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.680
increased formation of tartar,

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.520
an impairment
of taste sensation,

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.720
and occasionally damage

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.480
to the inner lining
of your mouth,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.200
whereas if anything,
drinking green tea

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.880
appears to have
good side effects.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.160
Tea consumption is associated
with living a longer life

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.560
thanks to links
to less cardiac death,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.960
heart disease, stroke,
and type 2 diabetes-

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.600
and not just by a little.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.160
A three cup (720 ml)
a day increase

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.080
in tea consumption per day

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.560
is associated with
a 24 percent decreased risk

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.520
of premature death
from all causes put together,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.720
the equivalent of adding
about two years

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.600
onto your lifespan.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.440
The longevity link extends
to both green tea and black,

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.080
though the per cup effect
appears greater with green.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.960
If you compare
the anti-microbial efficacy

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.280
of green tea and chlorhexidine
mouth rinses

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.600
against the bacteria
associated with tooth decay

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.200
in children with severe
early childhood cavities,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.960
this study found
that a green tea mouth rinse

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.640
was superior to chlorhexidine,

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.560
but this study
found the opposite.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.080
Chlorhexidine wiped out
like 95 percent

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.160
of the decay bacteria,

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.800
versus more like 70 percent
for green tea,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.120
but just rinsing
with water alone

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.000
can cut levels in half.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.000
So, in terms of
protecting teeth,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.800
the effectiveness of green tea
as a mouth rinse agent

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:04.760
has not been proven.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.600
But what about for bad breath?

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.360
The effect of green tea
on halitosis

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.920
has evidently been well known
from early times,

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.200
perhaps due
to the deodorizing activity

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.000
of certain
antioxidant polyphenols.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.560
But you don't really know
until you put it to the test.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.480
This study found that sucking
on green tea tablets

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.920
reduced stinky breath compounds,

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.880
but who sucks on tea tablets?

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.040
Here, green tea was compared
to breath mints, chewing gum,

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.920
and parsley oil,
and green tea seemed to help,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.600
but did not reach
statistical significance.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.720
But again, it wasn't drinking
or swishing with green tea,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.920
but rather
some green tea powder

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.600
sprinkled on people's tongues.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.280
This study used actual tea

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.120
and found
that one minute later,

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.600
swishing with green tea
was no better

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.760
than swishing with water.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.040
Okay, but what about chronic use
over time?

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.560
This is the study
I had been looking for.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.280
A randomized, double-blind,
placebo-controlled trial

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.160
in which people
rinsed their mouths twice a day

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.160
for a month
with a green tea mouthwash

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.040
or a similar looking/tasting
placebo mouthwash.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.640
And at the end of the month,

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.240
the stinky bad breath compounds
were reduced nearly 40 percent

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.800
in the green tea group versus
closer to only 10 percent

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.720
in the placebo group.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.240
Bottom line:
a systematic review

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:27.760
on the effect of the tea plant

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.360
on decreasing
the level of halitosis

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.040
concluded
that green tea mouthwash

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.760
can indeed reduce bad breath,

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.040
though they don't feel
the evidence

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.240
is sufficiently robust
for dentists

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.320
to start recommending it
on its own

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.520
due to lack of enough
randomized clinical studies.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.240
But green tea mouthwash can be
a good treatment of choice

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.040
beside other halitosis
treatments,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.120
like tongue scraping,

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:59.680
to achieve
better clinical results.

